00:00:08.003 --> 00:00:10.860
Helen here with some thoughts
about "Stranger Things".

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:13.200
The content overlords at
Netflix finally dropped

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.840
the end of season four, and
according to popular opinion,

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.880
the Duffer brothers stuck the landing.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:19.710
But while the show has
been praised for maturing

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:22.560
with season four, it hasn't
completely shaken criticism

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.050
that it relies too heavily on nostalgia.

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:27.750
Some see it as little more
than a gumbo of references

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:29.220
aimed at grown up '80s kids.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:31.620
But in recent seasons it's
introduced plot points

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.810
that confront the darker
side of the 1980s,

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:35.070
and nostalgia in general,

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:36.840
choosing to actually examine the past

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:38.070
instead of just serving up more

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:40.710
of the warm and fuzzy
nostalgia we've come to expect.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:42.060
And yet, these two approaches,

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:43.530
the comforting and the critical,

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:45.660
are also diametrically opposed,

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:47.790
which makes us wonder
can "Stranger Things"

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:50.250
subvert the very nostalgia it also evokes?

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:51.917
Let's take a look in
this Wisecrack Edition

00:00:51.917 --> 00:00:54.300
can "Stranger Things" nostalgia grow up?

