That's betterH-E-L-P.com/wisecrack.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:20.220
And now back to the show.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:21.660
Now the Duffer brothers have been open

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:23.250
about how much they're
borrowing from the films

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.620
of yesteryear, specifically,
Stephen Spielberg's.

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:26.453
And they're not subtle.

00:02:26.453 --> 00:02:28.320
"Stranger Things" has been
filled with references

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:31.200
to 1980s movies from the
jump, and has always shown

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:32.670
just how saturated the kids are

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:33.900
in the decade's pop culture.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:35.610
- What if the bad people kidnapped her?

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:37.140
What if they're forcing her to say this?

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:38.861
- [Nancy] I need you to answer.

00:02:38.861 --> 00:02:40.650
- It's like Lando Calrissian.

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:41.483
Don't answer.

00:02:41.483 --> 00:02:43.170
- [Helen] "Stranger Things"
is hardly the first TV show

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:44.520
to peddle in nostalgia

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:47.310
but its salience today may
be indicative of our times.

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:49.177
Scholar Svetlana Boym calls nostalgia

00:02:49.177 --> 00:02:51.510
"an incurable modern condition,"

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:54.157
describing its ubiquity
as "a defense mechanism

00:02:54.157 --> 00:02:56.197
"in a time of accelerated rhythms of life

00:02:56.197 --> 00:02:57.720
"and historical upheavals."

00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:59.700
That is to say, when our world is changing

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:02.340
and the present feels
precarious, like, um,

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:03.840
right this (beep) second,

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.060
we collectively experience
what Boym describes

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.077
as, "a yearning for a different time.

00:03:08.077 --> 00:03:09.667
"The time of our childhood,

00:03:09.667 --> 00:03:11.790
"the slower rhythms of our dreams."

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:14.670
She noted way back in
2001 that American culture

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:17.520
was becoming increasingly self-referential

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.830
as it sought solace in a simpler past.

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:22.380
While the prevalence of
nostalgic content makes sense,

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:25.110
many remain skeptical about
how "Stranger Things" uses it.

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:27.450
Early on the New York Times
labeled "Stranger Things"

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:30.420
as "'80s karaoke," and
while that's pretty harsh,

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:32.910
especially if "Don't Stop
Believing" is your show stopper,

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:34.620
the show is definitely preoccupied

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:36.720
with that decade's
surface level aesthetic.

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:39.090
There's the fashion, the
toys, the technology,

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:40.320
the public spaces.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.540
By saturating itself
with all of the dressings

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:45.718
of the '80s, the show fully
makes use of its period setting,

00:03:45.718 --> 00:03:47.880
albeit in a way that often isn't relevant

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:48.900
to the actual plot.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:51.540
Its focus on children and
adolescents also makes it ripe

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:53.880
for some of the strongest
pop culture associations

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:55.080
of the 1980s.

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:57.000
As marketing scholars William J. Havlena

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:58.440
and Susan L. Holak note,

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:01.020
we tend to have the strongest
nostalgic associations

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:03.660
with media and pop culture
from our adolescence,

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.580
which is presumably true for the Gen X'ers

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:07.620
and older millennials
who form a sizable chunk

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:08.940
of Netflix's audience.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:11.100
And each moment in the
'80s of "Stranger Things"

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:12.690
is filled with all of the cool stuff

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:14.070
we associate with the decade,

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:17.010
creating an idealized version
that all of us can pine for.

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:18.750
And you could argue this idealism

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:20.460
has grown stronger each season.

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:23.280
Season two gave us a "Gremlin's"
riff with Dustin and Dart

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:26.250
and
the
whole
small-town-kids-fight-shady-invading-Russians

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:28.680
thing in season three is
straight up "Red Dawn."

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:31.500
This left season four feeling
disjointed in its potpourri

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:34.560
of genre tropes, lurching
from zany Spielbergian comedy

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:36.210
to a "Silence of the Lambs" ripoff.

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:38.850
By overtly mirroring movies
in its cinematography

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:40.740
and narrative, the show is engaging

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:43.410
in some good old fashioned intertextuality

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:45.690
or in other words, it's
creating a relationship

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:48.120
by mapping itself onto
an already existing work

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:49.200
with the same tone.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:51.810
When Robin and Nancy's visit
with an accused serial killer

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:53.978
reminds us so vividly of
"Silence of the Lambs,"

00:04:53.978 --> 00:04:57.120
we're reminded how we felt when
we first saw Anthony Hopkins

00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:59.520
creep it up, and it puts
us in that head space.

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:00.865
No Chianti required.

00:05:00.865 --> 00:05:01.710
(Hannibal hissing)

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:04.350
And intertextuality engages
with nostalgic media

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:05.610
in a specific way.

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:08.700
Scholar Frederic Jameson observes
that in an age of remakes

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:10.440
and nostalgic oversaturation,

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:12.810
intertextuality displaces real history

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:16.110
by substituting fact for
aesthetic style, or pseudohistory.

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:18.607
He writes that, "the nostalgic
film was never a matter

00:05:18.607 --> 00:05:21.877
"of some old-fashioned
representation of historical content,

00:05:21.877 --> 00:05:23.497
"but instead approach the past

00:05:23.497 --> 00:05:25.177
"through stylistic connotation,

00:05:25.177 --> 00:05:28.950
"conveying pastness by the
glossy qualities of the image."

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:30.870
Referencing the media of the past

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:33.360
rather than the actual
history is the priority.

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:35.250
But not everyone watching
"Stranger Things" today

00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:36.840
was even alive in the '80s.

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:38.880
So what are they experiencing
when they indulge

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:40.980
in this virtual
reconstruction of the decade?

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:43.185
A little something scholar
Arjun Appadurai calls

00:05:43.185 --> 00:05:46.560
armchair nostalgia, or ersatz nostalgia.

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:49.087
That is, "nostalgia
without lived experience

00:05:49.087 --> 00:05:50.760
"or collective historical memory."

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:53.220
In his criticism of
nostalgia-tinged advertising

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:55.020
and merchandising which "Stranger Things"

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:58.260
has done its fair share of,
Appadurai argues that such media

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:00.967
creates nostalgia "for
things that never were.

00:06:00.967 --> 00:06:04.147
"This imagined nostalgia thus
inverts the temporal logic

00:06:04.147 --> 00:06:06.787
"of fantasy and creates much deeper wants

00:06:06.787 --> 00:06:09.277
"than simple envy,
imitation, or greed could,

00:06:09.277 --> 00:06:10.920
"by themselves, invite."

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:13.590
Interestingly enough, a lot
of young people's nostalgia

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:15.420
for the world depicted
in "Stranger Things"

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:18.180
is actually based on
nostalgia for the 1980s movies

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:19.410
that they watched in their youth

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:21.360
which defined that decade in their minds.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:23.430
Thus a pseudohistory is born.

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:25.800
And that's the thing about pseudohistory.

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:27.630
It erases some of the uglier truths

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:29.100
of the time period depicted.

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:30.870
Sure, early seasons of "Stranger Things"

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:32.370
are hardly all fun in games.

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:34.200
There's a genuine darkness
that underscores it

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:35.100
from the get-go,

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:37.380
most obviously the
disappearance of a cute kid.

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:40.410
But that darkness is defined
by an unambiguous evil,

00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:43.050
rendering the conflict that
ensues almost childlike

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:44.040
in its simplicity.

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:45.930
There's no empathizing with the Demogorgon

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:48.390
no matter what your fondness
for Venus fly traps may be.

00:06:48.390 --> 00:06:50.160
Mind flayer is more of the same.

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:52.260
In this way, the darkness
of the Upside Down

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:53.820
in the first two seasons of the show

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:56.250
threatens the essential
lightness of the world above.

00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:57.720
And that threat only escalates

00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:00.120
as future seasons attempt
to complicate the balance.

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:02.760
Now there's a strong case
to be made that season three

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:04.650
of "Stranger Things"
marked a turning point

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:06.540
for the show's relationship with nostalgia

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:09.210
as it became less about reveling
in the commercial delights

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:12.330
of 1980s youth culture and
ventured closer to critique.

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:14.400
Obviously, there's still
plenty of production design

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:15.750
to make you pine for the '80s.

00:07:15.750 --> 00:07:16.770
Half the freaking season

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:18.630
is set at a shopping mall, after all.

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:21.270
But we learned that the mall
isn't all fun and games.

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:23.610
It's a sign of Reagan-era corporatization

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:26.670
that's displacing tons of
longstanding mom and pop shops.

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:29.220
When intern Nancy points this
out to the local newspaper,

00:07:29.220 --> 00:07:31.800
though, it's brushed off
as a matter of course,

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:34.411
unremarkable in pro-business,
Reagan-era America.

00:07:34.411 --> 00:07:36.630
(men laughing)

00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:39.480
What's more, unlike the
earlier ambient nostalgia,

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:41.220
this season's narrative revolves around

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:42.960
real anxieties from the '80s.

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:45.150
Most notably you have the Cold War

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:47.400
and the USSR's army plays a key role

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:49.920
as the show's first real human antagonist.

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:51.270
They want to use the Upside Down

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:52.830
to aid their Cold War agenda.

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:54.660
They set up camp in a giant lab

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:56.370
beneath Hawkins' Starcourt Mall

00:07:56.370 --> 00:07:58.800
with the help of a
colluding, power-hungry mayor

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:00.360
who puts on a patriotic fair

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:02.520
while facilitating a communist invasion.

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:05.370
The Cold War paranoia about
a covert Russian infiltration

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:08.280
aided and abetted by
politicians is thus realized,

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:09.450
as well as Cold War-era anxieties

00:08:09.450 --> 00:08:11.421
that the Soviets would use mind control

00:08:11.421 --> 00:08:13.890
to turn Americans into commie zombies.

00:08:13.890 --> 00:08:15.472
And though there's
plenty of fun to be had,

00:08:15.472 --> 00:08:17.280
it's arguably using nostalgia

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:19.770
for mildly darker purposes
than it has before

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:21.907
By making these elements
central to the narrative.

00:08:21.907 --> 00:08:23.850
"Stranger Things" is
seemingly acknowledging

00:08:23.850 --> 00:08:25.500
that the '80s might not really have been

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:28.050
as simple and chill as its
portrayal led us to believe.

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:28.883
Surprise!

00:08:28.883 --> 00:08:30.720
It marks a deliberate effort to engage

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:32.400
with the reality of the decade,

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:35.160
which was not just one big
game of Dungeons and Dragons.

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:37.590
But while season three tried
to address darker aspects

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:39.930
of the time, it didn't really pull it off.

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:42.390
In theory, the way "Stranger
Things" integrates issues

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:44.490
like corporatization
and Cold War anxieties

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:47.040
removed some of the shows
rose-tinted naivete.

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:49.470
But it also introduces
an element of whiplash

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:51.300
that undermines its own premise.

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:53.400
Nancy's newsroom critique
of corporatization

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:55.650
is quickly overshadowed
by all the fun antics

00:08:55.650 --> 00:08:56.483
enjoyed at the mall.

00:08:56.483 --> 00:08:58.290
And when it comes to
the Cold War narrative,

00:08:58.290 --> 00:09:01.177
the show ultimately settles
for simplicity over complexity,

00:09:01.177 --> 00:09:04.590
resolving nuanced, real world
issues by blaming everything

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:07.050
on the spooky, unambiguously evil Russians

00:09:07.050 --> 00:09:08.340
and their monster friends.

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:10.410
This invasion obviously, as we noted,

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:12.990
draws pretty explicitly
from the film "Red Dawn,"

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:15.120
invoking the same happy
nostalgia that it does

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:17.790
for those of us who watched
Spielberg films in the '90s.

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:20.040
By letting us revel in
the intertextuality,

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:21.330
we can immediately conclude

00:09:21.330 --> 00:09:23.310
that the kids are gonna triumph over evil.

00:09:23.310 --> 00:09:25.260
Intertextuality strikes again.

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:27.450
Maybe the show was trying
to critique that brand

00:09:27.450 --> 00:09:29.850
of feverish Cold War-era American media.

00:09:29.850 --> 00:09:32.940
But if so, it doesn't really
signpost that intention.

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:34.500
Mostly, it depicts the Cold War

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:36.420
with a false sense of simplicity,

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:37.980
just as warm and fuzzy nostalgia

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:40.237
suggests a desirable
simplicity of its own.

00:09:40.237 --> 00:09:41.331
(explosion banging)

00:09:41.331 --> 00:09:42.630
(mind flayer screaming)

00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:45.000
So, in trying to critique
its '80s setting,

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:46.680
season three still seems to conclude

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:48.780
that the time was simpler
than the present day.

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:50.610
But if season three tried and failed

00:09:50.610 --> 00:09:52.080
to critique '80s nostalgia,

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:54.540
then season four arguably embraces nuance

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:56.220
in order to further its commentary

00:09:56.220 --> 00:09:57.570
on nostalgia at large.

00:09:57.570 --> 00:09:59.070
The most obvious way it does so

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:01.320
is by invoking the Satanic Panic,

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:04.080
a real-world '80s moral
panic which sparked fears

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:06.486
that things like Dungeons and
Dragons, or heavy metal music

00:10:06.486 --> 00:10:09.273
were leading kids to
worship Satan, or like,

00:10:09.273 --> 00:10:10.860
do ritual murders.

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:12.720
Not coincidentally, both of those things

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:15.183
were enjoyed by social
outcasts and nonconformists.

00:10:15.183 --> 00:10:18.030
This added to the stigma
and other-ising of folks

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:19.800
who were already rendered pariahs

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:22.170
by the conservative social
norms of the decade.

00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:24.840
This was, after all, an era
that looked back quite fondly

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:26.970
on the rampant conformity of the '50s.

00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:29.340
And this clearly applies
to our protagonists

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:31.650
who are prime examples
of this sort of othering.

00:10:31.650 --> 00:10:34.110
Previously, we had seen our
plucky adolescent friends

00:10:34.110 --> 00:10:37.020
being bullied or excluded, but
this is the first real sense

00:10:37.020 --> 00:10:39.690
we get that nonconformity
in Reagan-era America

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:41.490
wasn't just socially unadvisable,

00:10:41.490 --> 00:10:44.070
it was potentially
criminal, as plenty of folks

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:46.410
convicted of dubious charges
during the Satanic Panic

00:10:46.410 --> 00:10:47.243
could tell you.

00:10:47.243 --> 00:10:49.410
And whether it's for their
interests, or their identity,

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:50.827
or their mental health issues,

00:10:50.827 --> 00:10:53.010
"Stranger Things" asks to care about folks

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:55.770
who are in danger of
'80s-style societal scorn.

00:10:55.770 --> 00:10:58.200
In a subversive twist,
it is only the outsiders

00:10:58.200 --> 00:10:59.670
who are able to believe the truth:

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:01.620
that a Dungeons and Dragons-style monster

00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:03.390
is on the hunt in Hawkins.

00:11:03.390 --> 00:11:05.970
Meanwhile, the jocks and cops
all lead to the conclusion

00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:09.240
that the freak, Eddie Munson
is responsible for the murders.

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:10.920
And during other moments on the show,

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:12.270
some of the trappings of the '80s

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:14.040
that might have once been celebrated

00:11:14.040 --> 00:11:15.540
become dark and troubling.

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:18.150
Like Eleven's horrible day
at the roller skating rink

00:11:18.150 --> 00:11:19.950
where a colorful, fun, retro trope

00:11:19.950 --> 00:11:21.910
turns completely haunting.

00:11:21.910 --> 00:11:24.433
(children laughing)

00:11:24.433 --> 00:11:26.100
(Eleven yelps)

00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:28.590
But arguably the show's best exploration

00:11:28.590 --> 00:11:29.760
of the perils of nostalgia

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:31.740
is in its preoccupation with trauma

00:11:31.740 --> 00:11:33.150
and mental health struggles.

00:11:33.150 --> 00:11:35.220
We know lots of people have
been talking about this,

00:11:35.220 --> 00:11:36.840
but we think it's worth diving deeper

00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:38.280
as we examine whether the show can

00:11:38.280 --> 00:11:40.380
successfully subvert its own nostalgia.

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:42.750
Most relevantly, there's
Hopper's backstory,

00:11:42.750 --> 00:11:43.890
which we finally learn.

00:11:43.890 --> 00:11:45.660
Like so many men of the era,

00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:48.510
his guilt and substance abuse
issues were rooted in trauma

00:11:48.510 --> 00:11:49.920
from his service in Vietnam.

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:51.073
- Ever since I was 18,

00:11:52.677 --> 00:11:55.140
and some letter of induction
in the mail Uncle Sam

00:11:55.140 --> 00:11:57.300
wants me to go fight some war in a jungle.

00:11:57.300 --> 00:11:59.010
- We learned specifically
that he was assigned

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:00.660
to the Agent Orange division.

00:12:00.660 --> 00:12:01.830
Ostensibly an herbicide

00:12:01.830 --> 00:12:04.506
to remove Vietnamese defenders'
tactical edge in the jungle,

00:12:04.506 --> 00:12:07.260
Agent Orange was also super toxic,

00:12:07.260 --> 00:12:09.780
causing widespread
disability and birth defects.

00:12:09.780 --> 00:12:12.030
The U.S. troops assigned
to the Agent Orange corp

00:12:12.030 --> 00:12:14.160
were kept in the dark
as to its true nature,

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:15.840
and many suffered its effects.

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:20.840
- The horror followed us, clung to us.

00:12:21.150 --> 00:12:23.670
- [Helen] Hopper blames himself
for his daughter's cancer,

00:12:23.670 --> 00:12:24.930
and for not warning his wife

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:27.120
about the risks caused by his exposure.

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:31.230
- We had a baby and she was,
um, she was born healthy.

00:12:31.230 --> 00:12:32.100
She was perfect.

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:35.790
You know, Sarah, and then she died.

00:12:35.790 --> 00:12:37.613
- His resulting descent
into substance abuse

00:12:37.613 --> 00:12:40.620
mirrors the experience of
many Vietnam War veterans.

00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:43.530
Many struggled with PTSD or
other mental health troubles,

00:12:43.530 --> 00:12:46.830
or were denied social services,
or even ended up unhoused.

00:12:46.830 --> 00:12:49.230
Here we see that not
everything about the '80s

00:12:49.230 --> 00:12:50.790
is something to be nostalgic for.

00:12:50.790 --> 00:12:53.040
Coupled with the very
real brutality of life

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:55.410
in a Soviet gulag, we also see the darker,

00:12:55.410 --> 00:12:57.570
less cartoonish reality of the Cold War.

00:12:57.570 --> 00:13:00.450
Then more abstractly,
there's El's season arc

00:13:00.450 --> 00:13:02.940
which sees her literally
reliving her younger life

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:04.650
under the care of Papa.

00:13:04.650 --> 00:13:06.810
And this serves as a good
reminder that not everyone

00:13:06.810 --> 00:13:08.520
would be stoked to return
to their childhood.

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:10.920
And in some of the season's
most powerful scenes

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:13.470
we watch as she delves into
her most horrific memories

00:13:13.470 --> 00:13:14.880
of a massacre at the lab.

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:17.820
The pain of reliving her
trauma feels equal and opposite

00:13:17.820 --> 00:13:19.890
to the nostalgic joy
viewers might have felt

00:13:19.890 --> 00:13:22.800
reliving their own '80s
childhoods, real or imagined,

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:24.240
in the earlier seasons.

00:13:24.240 --> 00:13:26.580
The trauma that El has
spent so long blocking

00:13:26.580 --> 00:13:28.980
from her memory feels
completely oppositional

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:31.680
to the whole idea of nostalgia
that the show was rooted in.

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:33.270
And it's probably the best example

00:13:33.270 --> 00:13:34.950
of the show's evolving maturity.

00:13:34.950 --> 00:13:37.980
Of course, trauma has always
undergirded "Stranger Things,"

00:13:37.980 --> 00:13:40.170
whether in Will's struggles
to get back to real life

00:13:40.170 --> 00:13:41.820
after his time on the Upside Down,

00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:43.350
or El's ongoing flashbacks

00:13:43.350 --> 00:13:44.970
about being separated from her mother.

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:47.040
But by focusing on it
throughout the season,

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:49.200
the show is taking what was subtext

00:13:49.200 --> 00:13:51.000
and making it central to the plot.

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:53.250
It's symptomatic of "Stranger
Things'" slow descent

00:13:53.250 --> 00:13:54.567
into a darker worldview.

00:13:54.567 --> 00:13:57.450
It's been slowly peeling
back its own nostalgia

00:13:57.450 --> 00:14:00.030
like an onion to reveal the
real trauma that lingers

00:14:00.030 --> 00:14:02.730
under even the happiest
memories of '80s childhood.

00:14:02.730 --> 00:14:04.377
And because we've come
to love the characters

00:14:04.377 --> 00:14:06.210
as tropes from '80s films,

00:14:06.210 --> 00:14:08.520
it takes us by surprise
when the show actually stops

00:14:08.520 --> 00:14:09.660
to examine their pain.

00:14:09.660 --> 00:14:12.270
And that examination, and the
growth that comes with it,

00:14:12.270 --> 00:14:14.850
subverts our ultimately
nostalgic view of them.

00:14:14.850 --> 00:14:17.340
It serves as a strong reminder
that looking backwards

00:14:17.340 --> 00:14:18.945
as a person, or as a nation,

00:14:18.945 --> 00:14:21.607
can be as painful as it is joyous.

00:14:21.607 --> 00:14:23.250
"Stranger Things" seems to be earnest

00:14:23.250 --> 00:14:25.020
in its attempts at critiquing nostalgia.

00:14:25.020 --> 00:14:26.703
But ultimately, these efforts lead

00:14:26.703 --> 00:14:29.580
to a sort of internal conflict of interest

00:14:29.580 --> 00:14:31.920
because alongside, say,
Hopper's discussion

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:33.360
of his time in Vietnam,

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:35.370
we still have the same nostalgic callbacks

00:14:35.370 --> 00:14:37.290
the series was originally beloved for.

00:14:37.290 --> 00:14:38.250
That's understandable.

00:14:38.250 --> 00:14:40.470
Their audience has come to
expect as much from the show.

00:14:40.470 --> 00:14:43.140
But it still creates a kind
of cognitive dissonance.

00:14:43.140 --> 00:14:46.380
In fact, in this season, the
show even goes kind of meta

00:14:46.380 --> 00:14:48.870
with its nostalgia,
featuring new flashbacks

00:14:48.870 --> 00:14:51.600
to past beloved moments
from earlier in the series.

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:54.163
This creates a sort of
double nostalgia for us;

00:14:54.163 --> 00:14:57.060
a nostalgia of remembering our
first time watching the show.

00:14:57.060 --> 00:14:59.040
You could argue that this
is a way for the creators

00:14:59.040 --> 00:15:01.560
to demonstrate just how
much things have changed,

00:15:01.560 --> 00:15:04.920
but arguably, it skirts
the edge of fan service.

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:06.690
The darker that "Stranger Things" goes,

00:15:06.690 --> 00:15:09.180
the more these twin threads
of celebratory nostalgia

00:15:09.180 --> 00:15:11.760
and critique of nostalgia
start to chafe together.

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:14.190
So in sum, we'd argue
that "Stranger Things"

00:15:14.190 --> 00:15:16.350
can't really subvert
the very same nostalgia

00:15:16.350 --> 00:15:17.537
it actively invokes.

00:15:17.537 --> 00:15:20.310
You can't have your nostalgia
cake and critique it, too.

00:15:20.310 --> 00:15:23.130
And let's face it, the show's
thrills and spills escapism

00:15:23.130 --> 00:15:24.750
is why we all got hooked to begin with.

00:15:24.750 --> 00:15:26.400
If season one had ground to a halt

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:28.620
to explore Joyce Byers'
experience of single,

00:15:28.620 --> 00:15:31.350
working-class motherhood,
would we have kept watching?

00:15:31.350 --> 00:15:32.460
There's not much room in the whimsical,

00:15:32.460 --> 00:15:35.970
Spielbergian paradigm for
examining the full-on complexity

00:15:35.970 --> 00:15:37.350
of real-world politics,

00:15:37.350 --> 00:15:39.330
or the nuanced and
highly-subjective effects

00:15:39.330 --> 00:15:41.010
that they have on us as human beings.

00:15:41.010 --> 00:15:43.500
But it can do compelling character work.

00:15:43.500 --> 00:15:44.970
And we'd argue that season four

00:15:44.970 --> 00:15:46.560
was the best example of that.

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:48.660
So "Stranger Things" mostly remains a show

00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:50.340
that creates intertextual nostalgia

00:15:50.340 --> 00:15:51.690
to make its audience remember,

00:15:51.690 --> 00:15:53.727
or pretend they remember, the '80s.

00:15:53.727 --> 00:15:55.800
And if it happens to do
so to commercial ends,

00:15:55.800 --> 00:15:56.633
then all the better

00:15:56.633 --> 00:15:58.560
to merchandise the (beep)
out of Eleven, right?

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:00.217
As historian Gary Cross puts it,

00:16:00.217 --> 00:16:03.277
"All nostalgias consist of
organizers and participants.

00:16:03.277 --> 00:16:06.003
"But today the organizers
increasingly are marketers,

00:16:06.003 --> 00:16:08.520
"and participants are consumers."

00:16:08.520 --> 00:16:10.710
That is to say, being nostalgic for an era

00:16:10.710 --> 00:16:12.480
naturally makes you more
likely to buy (beep)

00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:13.890
that reminds you of that era.

00:16:13.890 --> 00:16:16.020
Hey, let he who lives in a glass house

00:16:16.020 --> 00:16:17.370
cast the first Walkman.

00:16:17.370 --> 00:16:18.510
But this type of nostalgia

00:16:18.510 --> 00:16:21.000
has a potentially more
insidious side, too.

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:23.550
The imagined past, like
those invoked in the show,

00:16:23.550 --> 00:16:25.980
are also used to fuel
a political nostalgia

00:16:25.980 --> 00:16:27.930
that has real world consequences,

00:16:27.930 --> 00:16:29.580
like a right-wing yearning for a past

00:16:29.580 --> 00:16:31.287
where America was a place
full of faith, family,

00:16:31.287 --> 00:16:33.600
and straight people who owned guns,

00:16:33.600 --> 00:16:35.880
or the liberal nostalgia
for a world of tolerance

00:16:35.880 --> 00:16:37.170
and ethical capitalism.

00:16:37.170 --> 00:16:39.930
In both cases, this nostalgia
functionally distracts us

00:16:39.930 --> 00:16:41.730
from critically evaluating the present.

00:16:41.730 --> 00:16:44.820
It convinces us that the only
way forwards is backwards.

00:16:44.820 --> 00:16:46.748
It might not be a big deal
when this inspires us to,

00:16:46.748 --> 00:16:49.680
say, buy a two liter of
Coke with our Pizza Hut,

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:51.270
but it can potentially be harmful

00:16:51.270 --> 00:16:53.610
when it starts to shape
our political reality.

00:16:53.610 --> 00:16:55.800
Luckily, "Stranger Things"
seems content to stay out

00:16:55.800 --> 00:16:58.770
of the pesky boring politics
that constitute adult life,

00:16:58.770 --> 00:16:59.880
at least for now.

00:16:59.880 --> 00:17:01.050
But what do you think?

00:17:01.050 --> 00:17:02.250
Does "Stranger Things" succeed

00:17:02.250 --> 00:17:04.170
at critiquing the nostalgia
that it's based in,

00:17:04.170 --> 00:17:06.420
or was season four a
failed lab experiment?

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:07.590
Let us know in the comments.

00:17:07.590 --> 00:17:09.270
Thanks to our patrons
for all their support.

00:17:09.270 --> 00:17:11.730
And don't miss out on
our new Patreon perks.

00:17:11.730 --> 00:17:13.710
And remember you can
join us every Thursday

00:17:13.710 --> 00:17:14.940
for our weekly live stream.

00:17:14.940 --> 00:17:16.530
Throw fireworks at that subscribe button

00:17:16.530 --> 00:17:18.900
like you're vanquishing an
inter-dimensional monster

00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:20.700
and don't forget to ring that bell.

00:17:20.700 --> 00:17:22.560
As always, thanks for watching,

00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:24.870
and good luck making that deal with God.

00:17:24.870 --> 00:17:25.842
Later.

00:17:25.842 --> 00:17:28.675
